course: biology
lesson: 102
title: The African savanna area: hippos, meerkats, ostriches, wild dogs and hyenas
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The second half of the African area is full of animals that cartoons get wrong: the hippo is no ballerina, the ostrich does not hide its head, and the hyena is not just a sneaky thief.
explain: A hippo looks lazy and round, but it opens its mouth wide enough to fit a child, walks along the bottom of the pool because it cannot really swim, and guards its water so fiercely that it is one of the most dangerous animals to people in Africa.
explain: Next door the meerkats run a lookout: one stands on a rock on its back legs while the others dig for beetles and scorpions, and if an eagle comes the guard gives a special call and everyone dives into the burrow.
explain: Timon the meerkat and the giggling hyenas of The Lion King, and Gloria the hippo of Madagascar, are fun, but the real animals are cleverer and stranger than their cartoon copies.
fact: A hippo can open its mouth to about 150 degrees as a warning to rivals, and its long lower tusks grow all its life and are for fighting, because hippos eat grass.
fact: Hippos cannot really swim; they are so heavy that they sink, so they walk or bounce along the bottom and push up to breathe.
fact: Hippos ooze a red-orange liquid sometimes called «blood sweat», which is neither blood nor sweat but works as sunscreen and may help stop germs in cuts.
fact: The closest living relatives of hippos are whales and dolphins.
fact: In a meerkat group one animal at a time stands guard on a high spot, and its alarm calls tell the others whether danger comes from the sky, like an eagle, or from the ground, like a jackal.
fact: Meerkats have thin fur and dark skin on their bellies, so after cold desert nights they warm up by sunbathing belly to the sun; they also eat scorpions, often biting off the sting first.
fact: Ostriches do not bury their heads in sand; they lower their heads to turn their eggs in a ground nest or lie flat to hide, and a scared ostrich runs at up to about 70 kilometres per hour or kicks.
fact: The ostrich is the biggest living bird and lays the biggest egg, as heavy as about 20 to 24 hen's eggs, with a shell strong enough for a person to stand on.
fact: An ostrich's eye is about 5 centimetres across, bigger than its brain, and it has only two toes on each foot, which helps it run fast.
fact: African wild dogs, also called painted dogs, live in close packs, greet each other with squeaks and licks before a hunt, and share food with pups and with sick or old members.
fact: Warthogs have short necks and long legs, so they kneel on thick pads on their wrists to graze short grass, and they back into burrows so their tusks face out; Pumbaa in The Lion King is a warthog.
fact: Spotted hyenas giggle when nervous or excited and whoop to call the clan; they catch most of their own food, lions often steal from them, and their clans are led by females, which are bigger than males.
exercise: Why does a hippo open its huge mouth so wide? | It is warning a rival to keep away | It is very tired and wants to sleep | It is cooling its big teeth in the air | It is calling its baby over to feed
exercise: What does a hippo mainly eat? | Grass | River fish | Zebras | Crocodile eggs
exercise: How does a hippo move around under water? | It walks or bounces along the bottom | It swims fast like a dolphin | It floats on its back like an otter | It paddles with its tail like a fish
exercise: What is the hippo's red «blood sweat»? | A liquid that works as sunscreen | Real blood from tiny cuts | Ordinary sweat coloured red by the river mud | Paint the keepers put on
exercise: Which animals are the hippo's closest living relatives? | Whales and dolphins | Pigs and wild warthogs | Elephants and rhinos | Horses, donkeys and zebras
exercise: What is the job of a meerkat standing up on a rock? | It guards the group and calls if danger comes | It is showing off to the others | It is looking for a lost baby | It is trying to reach the ripe fruit hanging on a tree
exercise: Why does the guard meerkat have more than one alarm call? | Different calls tell an eagle from a jackal | It just likes to make a lot of noise | Each call is the name of a different meerkat in the group | The calls are songs it learned from birds
exercise: Why do meerkats sunbathe belly to the sun in the morning? | Thin fur and dark skin there warm them up fast | To get a brown tan that lasts for the rest of the year | To dry off after a swim in the river | To show their bellies to the eagles
exercise: What does an ostrich really do with its head down near the ground? | Turns its eggs or lies flat to hide | Buries its head in the sand from fear | Listens for worms under the sand | Sleeps standing on its head
exercise: About how many hen's eggs weigh as much as one ostrich egg? | About 20 to 24 | About 2 or 3 at most | About 200 of them | Exactly 6, like a box
exercise: What is surprising about an ostrich's eye? | It is bigger than its brain | It can see only in the dark of night | It has no colour in it at all | It is smaller than a garden pea
exercise: How do African wild dogs get ready for a hunt? | They greet each other with squeaks and licks | They sleep alone in separate dens | They howl together at the moon for an hour or more | They eat grass to get strong
exercise: Why does a warthog kneel to eat? | Its neck is short and its legs long | Its back legs are always tired | It is saying a little prayer before it eats | It digs for meat under the ground
exercise: Who leads a clan of spotted hyenas? | The females, which are bigger than males | The oldest and biggest male | A lion that adopts the whole clan as its own family | Nobody, each hyena lives alone
exercise: How do spotted hyenas mostly get their food? | They hunt most of it themselves | They only ever eat what the lions leave | They only eat fruit and roots | Keepers in the wild feed them
test: «hippo's just a big lazy cow, I could swim with it lol» What is right? | Never: hippos guard their water fiercely and are very dangerous | Fine, hippos are gentle grass-eaters | Fine, as long as it is not yawning | Fine, hippos cannot bite in water
test: In Disney's Fantasia, hippos in tutus dance across the water. What is real? | Hippos sink and walk or bounce on the bottom | Hippos float high and spin round and round on the surface | Hippos swim gracefully like ballerinas | Hippos cannot go into water at all
test: «look, that ostrich buried its head cos it's scared» What is true? | It is turning its eggs or hiding by lying flat | Yes, ostriches hide their heads when afraid | Yes, it is drinking water under the sand | No, it is eating stones to sharpen its beak
test: In The Lion King the hyenas only steal leftovers from lions. What is real? | Hyenas hunt most of their food, and lions often steal from them | Hyenas never hunt anything themselves, they just steal from lions | Hyenas only eat plants and berries | Lions always share their meat with hyenas
test: 4 ostriches walk past. How many toes do they have altogether? | 16 | 32 | 40 | 8
test: What would happen if an eagle flew over while the meerkats were digging? | The guard would call and they would dive into the burrow | The meerkats would stand still and wave | The guard would jump up and fight the eagle all on its own | Nothing, eagles never eat meerkats
test: «hyenas are laughing cos they're happy» What is right? | The giggle often means they are nervous or excited | Yes, they giggle at all the funny things they see | Yes, they laugh only after a big meal | No, hyenas make no sound at all
test: 3 hippos and 5 meerkats in the African area. How many tails? | 8 | 5 | 3 | 15
test: Why would a mother hippo's red skin liquid be useful on a hot day? | It works like sunscreen for her skin | It cools her down by turning to ice | It keeps flies away by smelling of flowers | It makes her look scary to lions
test: «warthogs walk into their holes head first, don't they» What is true? | They back in, so their tusks face out | Yes, head first, so they can dig deeper | They never go into holes at all | They sleep on top of the hole
test: A sick old wild dog cannot hunt any more. What will its pack do? | Share food with it | Chase it out of the pack | Leave it with the hyenas | Make it guard the pups only
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the locomotion of the hippopotamus in water? | It walks or bounds along the bottom, being too dense to swim in the ordinary sense | It swims with great speed by means of its tail | It floats upon the surface and does not submerge | It avoids water entirely and inhabits dry plains
exam: Identify the principal function of the red-orange secretion upon the skin of the hippopotamus. | It serves as a protection against the sun and possibly against infection | It is blood lost through numerous small wounds | It is sweat coloured by the mud of the river | It serves to attract a mate from a great distance across the river
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the ostrich lowering its head towards the ground. | It is turning its eggs or lying flat to conceal itself, and does not bury its head | It is burying its head in the sand from fear | It is drinking water from beneath the sand | It is sleeping in an inverted position
exam: State which sex holds authority within a clan of spotted hyenas. | The females, which exceed the males in size | The males, which exceed the females in size | Neither, the clan having no order of rank | An adopted lion of either sex
